Looking at this problem, I need to analyze the functional graph formed by the grid movement directions.

Key insight: Since each cell has exactly one outgoing direction, this forms a functional graph where each component has exactly one cycle. Robots moving deterministically will eventually enter and traverse cycles.

For collision analysis:
- Two robots starting at different cells will collide if they ever occupy the same cell at the same time
- The crucial observation is that two start positions are equivalent (lead to collision) if they reach the same (cycle_id, cycle_position) after the same number of steps

The solution:
1. Build the graph where each cell points to its next cell
2. For each cell, determine which cycle it eventually reaches and the cycle position it reaches
3. Group all cells by their equivalence class (which cycle they reach at which position)
4. For each equivalence class, at most one robot can be placed (to avoid collision)
5. Maximize black cells by placing robots on black cells in each class

Let me implement this solution:

